WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.080
Binary parsers deserve tests that focus on failure paths, not only happy paths.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:23.060
Useful tests include empty input, one-byte input, truncated headers, truncated payloads, oversized fields, inconsistent counts, unsupported versions, unexpected flags, boundary values, maximum allowed sizes, and values just beyond the limit.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.160
These tests make validation rules executable.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:29.740
Corpus tests are especially helpful for formats that evolve.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:35.320
A corpus can include known valid samples and known invalid samples, with expected outcomes.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:42.420
When the parser changes, the corpus helps preserve intentional behavior and catches regressions in edge cases that are easy to forget.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:48.160
Fuzzing can support robustness testing by exercising unusual input combinations at scale.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:58.860
At a high level, fuzzing feeds many generated or mutated inputs into a parser and observes whether the parser crashes, hangs, leaks, or violates expected checks.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:05.560
It is not a replacement for design review, but it is a valuable way to discover assumptions that ordinary unit tests missed.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.740
Runtime sanitizers add another layer during testing.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:21.520
Address, undefined behavior, leak, and memory sanitizers can expose out-of-bounds access, use of uninitialized data, integer issues, and cleanup mistakes while the test suite and fuzzing harnesses run.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:26.700
Findings should be treated as signals that validation, arithmetic, or ownership needs attention.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:36.200
Static analysis can identify unchecked lengths, risky casts, suspicious pointer arithmetic, ignored return values, and calculations that may overflow.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:40.020
The best results come when analysis is combined with clear code structure.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.920
Tools are more useful when parser intent is explicit.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:51.340
Code review should trace every length, offset, count, allocation, and pointer access back to a validation rule.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:02:03.560
Reviewers should ask: where did this value come from, what range was allowed, could the calculation overflow, does the full range fit, what happens when the input is truncated, and how is partial state cleaned up.

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.700
That discipline turns review from style feedback into real parser assurance.
